@page "/"

<PageTitle>WellBore Architecture</PageTitle>

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC WellBore Architecture</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This microservice manages WellBore Architecture records: the structural description of a drilled path from the
    fluids above ground down through the well head, the surface sections, and the casing programme, together with
    identity assignments, feature assignments, and portable versioned backups. It persists to SQLite, enforces
    optimistic concurrency on every mutation, and exposes the same operations through a REST API, an MCP server, and
    a Blazor web application, so an architecture created or edited in one interface is immediately visible through
    the others.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">What is a WellBore Architecture</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A <b>WellBore Architecture</b> is the structural record for one drilled path: a name, a description,
                server-owned creation and modification timestamps, an optional <code>WellBoreID</code> linking it to
                the wellbore it describes, its identity and feature assignments, and the physical structure itself —
                a <b>well head</b>, any fluids sitting above ground level, an ordered list of <b>surface
                sections</b>, and an ordered list of <b>casing sections</b> with their elements and open-hole
                intervals. Most engineering quantities are stored as probabilistic (Gaussian or scalar) drilling
                properties rather than single numbers, and each construction object exposes a <code>Realize()</code>
                method that samples them down to a deterministic snapshot when a single realistic value is needed.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                All persisted values use SI units, and every depth is stored in metres referenced to the WGS84
                vertical datum; MSL, RKB, well-head, and ground-level depth references are presentation concerns
                handled by the web pages and converted back to WGS84 before anything is saved.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The <b>fluids above ground level</b> describe the column of air and water the tubulars are exposed to
                above the well head, each layer a fluid type — <code>Air</code> or <code>Water</code> — with the
                depth down to which it extends. On land that column is air only; offshore it is typically water up
                to sea level and air above it. A fixed-platform well can invert part of that picture: where a
                conductor pipe runs inside a platform pile, the tubular is exposed to air from the ground line
                upward even though the installation as a whole sits offshore, so the fluid list is what actually
                distinguishes an onshore, an offshore, and a through-pile completion rather than any single flag on
                the architecture.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">How an architecture links to a WellBore</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                A WellBore Architecture is not the same thing as a <b>WellBore</b>. The <b>WellBore</b> microservice
                owns the drilled-path record itself — its well and rig associations, sidetrack topology, trajectory,
                and survey stations — while this service owns the structural description of what was built along
                that path: the casing programme, the surface facilities, and the well head that separates them.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                The link runs one way, through the optional <code>WellBoreID</code> on the architecture: an external
                reference this service validates but never blocks a write on, so an architecture can be planned and
                stored before, or independently of, the wellbore record it will eventually be attached to. The
                <b>Contextual Data</b> menu resolves that reference — and the Well, Cluster, Field, and Rig records
                reachable from it — for display, described further below.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Because the link is a reference rather than a one-to-one binding, the same wellbore can legitimately
                have <b>several</b> architecture records pointing at it at once — one per hole section as it is
                drilled, a <b>planned</b> architecture describing how the wellbore is expected to look once
                complete, and a separate <b>as-built</b> (actual) architecture recording what was really run, updated
                as drilling and completion progress. Nothing in the schema itself tells these apart: it is the
                <b>feature</b> assignments — for example a category such as "Architecture Status" with options like
                Planned, Actual, or a per-section tag — that let callers and the web application distinguish which
                architecture record is which for a given wellbore.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Top and downhole architecture, split at the well head</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every architecture is organized around its <b>well head</b>, the fixed point that separates what sits
                above it from what hangs below it. The well head itself carries a depth, minimum and maximum outside
                diameter, and the casing- and tubing-hanger depths at which downhole strings are landed.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Above the well head is the <b>top architecture</b>: any fluids sitting above ground level, and the
                ordered <code>SurfaceSections</code> list — riser joints, diverters, and other above-ground or
                near-surface elements, each built from a network of side connectors and side elements — sorted top
                to bottom down to the well head.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Below the well head is the <b>downhole architecture</b>: the ordered <code>CasingSections</code>
                list, sorted top to bottom starting at the well head, each with a top depth, length, and
                top-of-cement depth, its casing section elements, a size table of length and diameter pairs, and the
                open-hole interval that continues from where the previous casing section — or the ground level for
                the first one — left off.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Identities and features</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Every architecture can carry any number of user-defined <b>identities</b> — symbolic references such
                as an operator architecture name or an external design reference — each with an architecture-specific
                value, and any number of <b>feature</b> assignments drawn from user-defined categories and options,
                optionally exclusive within a category and optionally bounded by a from/to date.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                The identity and feature <i>catalogs</i> are managed on the
                <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureIdentities">Identities</MudLink> and
                <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureFeatures">Features</MudLink> pages,
                while a specific architecture's assignments are added, replaced, or removed one at a time from the
                <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitecture">WellBore Architecture</MudLink>
                page — a definition or option cannot be deleted while an architecture still references it.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Contextual data and calculators</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The web application's <b>Contextual Data</b> menu gives read access to the records an architecture's
        <code>WellBoreID</code> chains up to: <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBore">WellBore</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/Well">Well</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/Cluster">Cluster</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/Field">Field</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/Rig">Rig</MudLink> — each backed by its own microservice and
        shown here through reusable Razor components, so an architecture can be understood in its wellbore, well,
        field, and drilling-program context without leaving this application.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Calculators</b> menu exposes the same synchronous, stateless evaluations used elsewhere in OSDC —
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/FieldCartographicConverter">Cartographic Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/EarthVerticalDatumCalculation">Vertical Datum Conversion</MudLink>,
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Earth Gravity Evaluation</MudLink>, and
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/EarthMagneticFieldCalculation">Earth Magnetic Field
        Evaluation</MudLink> — for one-off conversions and lookups while working an architecture, without switching
        to another site.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Backup and restore</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Backups are logical, portable JSON, not a raw database copy. From the
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>
        page, export <b>all</b> architectures or an ordered <b>selection</b> into one schema-versioned document that
        carries the complete architectures together with only the identity definitions and feature categories/options
        they actually reference — enough to reproduce the architectures on another installation without pulling in
        unrelated catalog clutter. The <code>WellBoreID</code> travels as an external reference and is not resolved
        during export.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Restoring the same document back offers a <b>conflict policy</b> — fail if any architecture UUID already
        exists, or replace matching architectures while creating the rest — and a <b>catalog policy</b> — map onto
        existing local identity/feature definitions, or create the missing ones locally, with normalized-name
        mapping across different UUIDs available only when explicitly allowed. Validation, catalog mapping, and
        every architecture write happen inside one SQLite transaction, so a restore of many architectures either
        commits completely or leaves the database untouched.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Data integrity and monitoring</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Architecture updates, deletes, and individual identity/feature assignment, surface-section, and
        casing-section changes use <b>optimistic concurrency</b>: every mutation must echo back the
        <code>LastModificationDate</code> from the caller's latest read, and a stale request is rejected rather than
        silently overwriting a concurrent change. Deleting a catalog definition or option still in use by an
        architecture is likewise rejected. Surface sections and casing sections can each be reordered independently
        of the rest of the architecture, addressed by a stable <code>ComponentID</code> rather than by array
        position.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Because <code>WellBoreID</code> points at another service, WellBore Architecture also offers read-only
        <b>external-reference validation</b> for one architecture and a bounded <b>audit</b> across all or selected
        architectures, each reference reported as <code>Valid</code>, <code>Invalid</code>, or
        <code>Unavailable</code> so a dependency outage is never confused with a genuinely broken reference. The
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/StatisticsWellBoreArchitecture">Usage Statistics</MudLink> page
        reports per-endpoint request counts for this service replica.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitecture">WellBore Architecture</MudLink> to
        browse, search, create, edit, and remove architectures, edit the well head, above-ground fluids, surface
        sections, and casing sections, and manage identity and feature assignments. Manage the shared catalogs on
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureIdentities">Identities</MudLink> and
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureFeatures">Features</MudLink>, and move data
        between installations on
        <MudLink Href="/WellBoreArchitecture/webapp/WellBoreArchitectureBackupRestore">Backup and Restore</MudLink>.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <b>Contextual Data</b> and <b>Calculators</b> menus give access to the related records and evaluations
        described above, and the <b>Monitoring</b> menu links to the usage statistics page. All numeric fields
        follow the unit system selected for the page through the OSDC unit-reference system.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same architecture data is reachable through several interfaces built on the same underlying store, so it
        can be managed interactively, scripted, embedded in other Blazor applications, or wired into AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for browsing, editing, backing up, and
                    restoring architectures, inspecting contextual data, running calculators, and reviewing usage
                    statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/WellBoreArchitecture/api</code> for architecture CRUD, details,
                    well-head/topology updates, surface- and casing-section add/update/delete/reorder, identity and
                    feature catalog management, targeted assignment mutations, external-reference validation and
                    audit, and batch export/restore, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI at
                    <code>/WellBoreArchitecture/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any
                    HTTP client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at
                    <code>/WellBoreArchitecture/api/mcp</code> (and WebSocket at
                    <code>/WellBoreArchitecture/api/mcp/ws</code>), publishing every non-statistics REST operation —
                    architecture CRUD, section and topology mutations, catalog management, external-reference
                    validation and audit, and batch export/restore — plus connectivity <code>ping</code>, with
                    strict, closed input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can query, create,
                    update, and back up architectures directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the WellBore Architecture, catalog, backup/restore, and section-edit
                    pages are published as the <code>OSDC.Drilling.WellBoreArchitecture.WebPages</code> NuGet
                    package, so other Blazor applications can embed the same unit-aware controls instead of
                    re-implementing this UI — the same way this application embeds the WellBore, Well, Cluster,
                    Field, and Rig web pages for its own Contextual Data menu.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
